const MOVIES_MCUDC = [{"num":680,"t":"Batman","y":"1966","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"\"Campy, goofy '60s movie may not appeal to today's kids,\" per Common Sense Media. Fistfights are punctuated with onscreen \"POW!\" graphics, a comically fake shark bites at Batman's leg while he dangles from a helicopter, and viewers are briefly led to think Batman has been blown up before he's shown to be fine; the Penguin smokes with a cigarette holder, and Batman goes on a date that includes a kiss. Positive: CSM notes the film draws a cartoonish but clear line between heroes and villains, though it flags weak overall role-modeling and a dated gag mocking overweight women.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-1966","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Comedy"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":681,"t":"Superman","y":"1978","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Consent issues in sweeping, nostalgic superhero adventure.\" Concerns: extensive peril without graphic blood (an exploding planet, an earthquake, a plane crash), plus real consent problems played as humor -- Superman kisses an unconscious Lois and uses X-ray vision on her without permission, and a soldier jokes about giving \"mouth-to-mouth\" to an unconscious woman. Mild profanity (\"bulls--t,\" \"damn,\" \"ass\") and social smoking/drinking appear throughout. Positive: good against evil and personal integrity are central themes, with Superman modeling humility and honesty even as the film's women are frequently rescued or objectified.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/superman-the-movie","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":682,"t":"Superman II","y":"1980","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Witty, action-packed superadventure with big themes.\" Concerns: Lois is nearly crushed to death and Superman battles three escaped Kryptonian villains amid heavy destruction; a powerless Clark Kent is beaten by a bully and left with a bloody nose, which can startle younger viewers; scattered profanity (\"hell,\" \"damn,\" \"s--t,\" \"son of a bitch\"); and a discreetly depicted scene implying Lois and Clark's relationship is consummated. Positive: Superman is held up as \"the ultimate superhero role model,\" sacrificing his powers for love and then reclaiming his duty for the greater good.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/superman-ii","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":683,"t":"Superman III","y":"1983","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"\"Outdated superhero movie may interest older kids,\" per Common Sense Media. Richard Pryor's comic-relief hacker Gus Gorman gets comically drunk to break into a restricted computer room, and a red-kryptonite-corrupted Superman turns cruel, vandalizes a bar, and spends a night with a seductive villainess; fights between super-beings involve missiles and lightning bolts but no blood. Positive: Superman's good nature ultimately wins out over his corrupted alter ego, and his integrity is restored by the story's end.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/superman-iii","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Comedy"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":684,"t":"Supergirl","y":"1984","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"\"Female superhero can't save this '80s clunker,\" says Common Sense Media. The villainous sorceress Selena and her minions drink and smoke, occasional \"s--t\" and \"Jesus\" appear as exclamations, a mentor character dies in a non-graphic scene, and Supergirl shares some kisses with a love interest; comic-book battles involve knives and a runaway amusement-park ride but no blood or gore. Positive: Supergirl is a conscientious, duty-driven role model, offering a rare female-led superhero story where good triumphs over evil.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/supergirl-1984","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Fantasy"],"studio":"Sony Pictures","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":685,"t":"Superman IV: The Quest for Peace","y":"1987","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"\"Terrible '80s sequel has violence, nuclear weapons,\" per Common Sense Media. Superman battles the powerful Nuclear Man across space and an erupting volcano in growling, brawling clashes that can startle younger kids, and the film repeatedly references the destruction of Krypton and the loss of Superman's parents; a character named Lacy tries to seduce Clark Kent, and adults smoke cigars. Positive: the story stresses that even a well-intentioned goal like world peace is too big for one person to achieve alone, and champions environmental and journalistic integrity alongside Superman's heroism.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/superman-iv-the-quest-for-peace","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":686,"t":"Batman","y":"1989","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Stylish, violent, and dark superhero adventure,\" per Common Sense Media. The film features numerous shoot-outs and killings, including a quill pen jabbed into a man's throat and another character electrocuted to death, plus flashbacks to young Bruce Wayne watching his parents murdered; street prostitutes appear in a crime-ridden city scene, the Joker makes crude advances on Vicki Vale, sex between Bruce and Vicki is implied, and there's cigar/cigarette smoking and profanity including \"s--t.\" Positive: CSM notes the film blurs hero and villain, with Batman's vigilante justice as much a factor in his actions as genuine heroism.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-1989","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":687,"t":"Batman Returns","y":"1992","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"\"A darkly violent take on the comic-book hero,\" per Common Sense Media. The sequel is notably darker than the original, with lots of gunfire and carnage, a man shoved from a window, a close-range shooting, an enemy set on fire, and the Penguin biting at a man's nose; the Penguin's crude double entendres about Catwoman and a Batman-Catwoman fight staged like foreplay add sexual undertones, alongside heavier themes of child abandonment and corporate corruption. Positive: Batman is shown as brave and trying to do the right thing, though CSM flags that he crosses a line by killing rather than just capturing his opponents.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-returns","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":688,"t":"Batman: Mask of the Phantasm","y":"1993","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"\"Tamer than live-action, but still moody, violent.\" This noir-tinged Batman feature includes real death (including a parent's), gunfights, explosions, blood, and unsettling scenes of corpses and a menacing Joker, plus some social drinking. Positive: Batman is shown as a hero who refuses to kill even when consumed by loss, and Andrea is a genuinely capable, strong female lead, though the film's revenge plot complicates its moral message.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-mask-of-the-phantasm","genre":["Animation","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":689,"t":"Batman Forever","y":"1995","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Flashy live-action superhero tale has violence, vengeance,\" per Common Sense Media. Numerous fight scenes mix gunfire, fisticuffs, and explosions, and flashbacks show Bruce Wayne's father shot dead on the street and Dick Grayson watching his family killed at the circus; there's suggestive banter between Bruce and Chase, Two-Face's assistants wear revealing lingerie, and the Riddler brags about a device that taps into people's fantasies. Positive: CSM finds little here, noting the characters are \"too cartoonish\" to be real role models, and that Batman kills opponents rather than bringing them to justice.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-forever","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":690,"t":"Batman & Robin","y":"1997","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"\"Weak superhero movie has lots of innuendo, some violence,\" per Common Sense Media. Hand-to-hand combat, gunshots, and a hockey-stick fight fill the action, plus a motorcycle race that nearly turns fatal; practically everything Poison Ivy says is sexual innuendo or double entendre, the camera lingers on Batgirl's costumed body, and there's champagne at a gala, cigar smoking, and homeless characters sharing alcohol. Positive: CSM rates both positive messages and role models as very low for this campy, merchandise-heavy entry.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-robin","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":691,"t":"Steel","y":"1997","la":"12+","ca":"Not on CSM; substitute source used","full":"Not reviewed by Common Sense Media, so this draws on IMDb's parents' guide and Kids-In-Mind instead. Shaquille O'Neal's weapons-engineer hero opens with a Gulf War flashback where soldiers are shot and blown up, and Steel himself takes a bullet to the chest; later he brawls with gang members using laser weapons and regular guns amid explosions, falling debris, and near-misses with trains and vehicles, with some blood shown. Positive: Steel refuses to let his invented weapons fall into criminal hands and uses his engineering genius to protect his community rather than profit from it.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steel_(1997_film)","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":692,"t":"Batman & Mr. Freeze: SubZero","y":"1998","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"\"Animated '90s Batman tale has cartoon violence, peril.\" Mr. Freeze's quest to save his cryogenically frozen wife brings gunfights, knife fights, a car crash, a polar bear attack, and a fiery oil-rig climax where Batgirl is held captive, plus a black-tie scene with cocktails. Positive: despite cartoonish exaggeration, the film gives each character real stakes and shows Batman and Robin working together under pressure to save an innocent life.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-mr-freeze-subzero","genre":["Animation","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":693,"t":"Batman Beyond: Return of the Joker","y":"2000","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Joker's back in dark animated tale with cartoon violence.\" One of the franchise's darkest entries: characters are shot and killed, gas bombs and laser guns fly, and a flashback shows a young Robin kidnapped, tied down, and tortured by the Joker and Harley Quinn until he's driven to madness, alongside champagne drinking and the Joker's pipe-smoking. Positive: the story is really about flawed heroes trying to atone for old mistakes, though the film offers little else in the way of uplifting messaging.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-beyond-return-of-the-joker","genre":["Animation","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":694,"t":"X-Men","y":"2000","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"\"Comic-book adaptation has brains, brawn, and style.\" Mostly bloodless but visceral fights between mutants (fisticuffs, stabbing, a character liquefied) anchor this origin story, with infrequent strong language, some kissing, a mostly-unclothed blue mutant, and adults smoking cigars and drinking. Positive: the film's throughline is that peaceful coexistence and tolerance beat prejudice and conflict, with Professor X and his students modeling integrity even as Magneto raises real philosophical questions about acceptance.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/x-men","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":695,"t":"X2","y":"2003","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Fun but violent sequel has peril, cursing.\" Colonel Stryker's forces raid Xavier's school as Wolverine claws soldiers and a mutant pins the president with a knife; there's a scene implying forced sexual coercion, plus profanity (\"s--t,\" \"ass,\" \"d--k\") and teens smoking. Positive: Xavier's steady care for his students and the X-Men's loyalty and bravery model that embracing what makes you different can drive real progress.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/x2-x-men-united","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":696,"t":"Catwoman","y":"2004","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"CSM's blunt verdict: \"this movie is kitty litter.\" Beyond the critical drubbing, parents should know it features comic-book action violence, peril, robbery, and murder, plus a pervasive sensuality with implied sex and references to adultery; a character drinks, and a beauty product is depicted as literally addictive. CSM doesn't flag strong positive messages or role models here -- it's a rare miss for the superhero genre.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/catwoman","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":697,"t":"Batman Begins","y":"2005","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Smart and entertaining, but also very violent,\" per Common Sense Media. Hard, fast violence from martial arts and shootings is shot in dark, sometimes scary imagery, and the film revisits the murder of Bruce Wayne's parents; mild profanity (\"damn,\" \"hell,\" \"ass\") appears alongside drinking, smoking, and a hallucinogenic-drug plot device, with only minimal romance (one kiss). Positive: Batman is framed as fighting for justice and fairness even though his methods sometimes mirror the criminals he opposes, ultimately affirming that \"there are heroes... willing to sacrifice themselves for the greater good.\"","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-begins","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":698,"t":"Elektra","y":"2005","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"CSM's tagline sums it up as a \"violent comic book movie with lingerie costumes,\" recommending age 14+. The martial-arts violence is graphic for a PG-13, with numerous fights and character deaths, though language and sexual content stay comparatively restrained (just a couple of kisses). Positive: Elektra ultimately chooses to protect an innocent father and daughter over completing her assassin contract, a redemption arc the review says the film doesn't fully earn dramatically.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/elektra","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":699,"t":"Fantastic Four","y":"2005","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"\"Standard superhero fare; sexual innuendo, fantasy violence.\" Comic-book-style action has the team body-slamming, punching, and zapping each other, plus a bridge car pileup and a man catching fire while snowboarding; mild profanity (\"ass,\" \"goddamn,\" \"hell\"), suggestive dialogue, and partial nudity (a woman in only a jacket, Sue in undergarments) round out the concerns. Positive: Sue is the team's voice of reason and Reed models maturity, with the film urging viewers to work together and value each other's differences -- even if the story doesn't always live up to it.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/fantastic-four","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":700,"t":"Superman Returns","y":"2006","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Thrilling but violent, tense return for the Man of Steel,\" per Common Sense Media. The film opens with a fiery vehicle crash and includes explosions, earthquakes, a giant wave, a bank robbery, and a sequence where a henchman stabs and beats Superman; mild language (\"damn,\" one s-word) appears, and in a bar scene Jimmy drinks beer while Lois starts to light a cigarette, which Superman discourages. Positive: Superman shows selfless heroism, confronting his own fear and vulnerability while still generously serving others.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/superman-returns","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":701,"t":"X-Men: The Last Stand","y":"2006","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"CSM recommends this violent trilogy-closer for age 13+. Characters are repeatedly stabbed, shot, and smashed with bloody gashes, a boy attempts self-harm, Mystique appears largely nude (with key areas covered), and mild profanity (\"bitch,\" \"hell,\" \"ass\") runs throughout. Positive: the X-Men model friendship and group unity, standing up for those persecuted simply for being different.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/x-men-the-last-stand","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":702,"t":"Fantastic Four: Rise of the Silver Surfer","y":"2007","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"\"Much better than the first; OK for tweens and up.\" Expect lots of explosions and crowds in jeopardy, including two character deaths (little blood shown), plus mild profanity (\"ass,\" \"crap,\" \"damn\"), a towel-clad shower scene, flirtatious \"fireproof lingerie\" dialogue, and background beer-drinking at a club. Positive: the team grows into real loyalty and teamwork, with one member learning to put the group's needs over his own ego.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/fantastic-four-rise-of-the-silver-surfer","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":703,"t":"Ghost Rider","y":"2007","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Devilish Nic Cage action flick isn't on fire,\" per Common Sense Media, which recommends it for age 13+. Constant comic-book violence includes motorcycle chases and chain-weapon fights, with villains turned \"gray and veiny\" as Johnny Blaze transforms into a flaming skull-headed Ghost Rider; language stays mild (\"s--t,\" \"hell,\" \"son of a bitch\") and drinking/smoking appear in several scenes. Positive: Johnny's original motive - trying to save his dying father - comes from a good place, even if his path afterward is more vigilante than role model.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ghost-rider","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":704,"t":"Superman: Doomsday","y":"2007","la":"12+","ca":"11+","full":"CSM calls it a \"violent cartoon spinoff with classic superhero,\" recommending it for kids 11 and up (parents lean toward 12+). Fights are intense enough that Superman visibly bleeds, characters die (including off-screen deaths of children and animals), and there are snapped-spine and gunshot deaths, plus scattered profanity like \"hell\" and \"ass.\" Positive: Superman risks everything to protect Earth, and the film digs into what really defines a hero beyond raw physical power.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/superman-doomsday","genre":["Animation","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":705,"t":"Batman: Gotham Knight","y":"2008","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"CSM describes this anthology as a \"dark, animated flock of Bat-stories has bloody violence.\" Expect graphic fights with blood gushing from wounds in a gory hospital sequence, a decapitation, gunfire, and explosions, though language stays minimal (just one \"ass\"). Positive: Batman consistently shows a strong moral compass and avoids killing even his enemies, while Alfred remains a loyal, wise guide who grounds him.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-gotham-knight","genre":["Animation","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":706,"t":"Iron Man","y":"2008","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"First MCU adventure has violence, Islamophobia,\" says CSM. Tony Stark's origin story brings extensive sci-fi war violence -- explosions, gunfire, a torture-and-escape sequence, and a child held at gunpoint -- plus scattered profanity (\"son of a bitch,\" \"damn\"), a smoked cigar, and a scene of Tony pressuring an assistant to dance with him despite her reluctance. Positive: Tony transforms from a careless arms-dealing playboy into someone who questions the morality of his own weapons, guided partly by his fellow hostage Yinsen's self-sacrifice, though CSM notes women are frequently objectified onscreen.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/iron-man","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":707,"t":"Justice League: The New Frontier","y":"2008","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"superheroes face Cold War paranoia in violent animated tale.\" Blood flows from injuries and dead bodies appear on screen, a boy is threatened with cult sacrifice by a knife-wielding fanatic, and there are two close-up fatal shootings along with mild profanity like \"son of a bitch.\" Positive: the League sets aside ego for teamwork and mutual respect even as the public and government turn against them, and the film closes on JFK's \"New Frontier\" speech linking pioneering spirit to confronting racism and prejudice.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/justice-league-the-new-frontier","genre":["Animation","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":708,"t":"The Dark Knight","y":"2008","la":"15+","ca":"15+","full":"\"Excellent sequel much darker, more violent than the first,\" per Common Sense Media. The film contains extensive, intense violence including shootings, stabbings, fistfights, and explosions, and the Joker's disturbing look and behavior can frighten viewers of any age; mild profanity (\"ass,\" \"hell,\" \"damn\") appears, along with background drinking at parties and bars and a scene implying intimacy between clothed characters. Positive: the film probes \"the rule of law versus the rule of force,\" contrasting Batman's morally gray vigilantism with the Joker's pure chaos and disregard for innocent life.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-dark-knight","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":709,"t":"The Incredible Hulk","y":"2008","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"\"Bloodier action than other comic book adventures,\" per CSM. Bruce Banner's fights as the Hulk are graphically destructive, with characters smashed and hurled through buildings, plus profanity including \"bitch,\" \"hell,\" and \"goddamn,\" some kissing in semi-clothed circumstances, and light cigar smoking. Positive: CSM calls the positive messaging thin -- the military hunts Banner hoping to weaponize him -- though Banner's own struggle to control his anger before it turns destructive offers a real lesson in self-restraint.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-incredible-hulk","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":710,"t":"Green Lantern: First Flight","y":"2009","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"dark animated superhero yarn will entertain older viewers.\" Villain Sinestro uses intimidation, torture, and murder with what CSM calls \"casual brutality\" unusually dark for the genre, plus mild swearing (\"ass,\" \"bastard,\" \"hell\"), an intergalactic bar scene, and a character drugged for interrogation. Positive: the film shows that teamwork, courage, and loyalty prevail over evil, and Hal Jordan's resistance to Sinestro's authoritarian ideology models standing up to tyranny.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/green-lantern-first-flight","genre":["Animation","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":711,"t":"Superman/Batman: Public Enemies","y":"2009","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"CSM sums it up as \"framed superheroes fight back -- and fight some more.\" Superman is framed for murder and hunted by nearly every hero and villain in the DC universe, leading to extensive, prolonged hand-to-hand combat and energy-blast battles (no blood or visible injuries, but bleeped profanity opens the film). Positive: both heroes model nobility and self-sacrifice -- Batman is willing to give his life to save the planet -- and the story explores the moral complexity of following orders versus doing what's right.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/superman-batman-public-enemies","genre":["Animation","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":712,"t":"Wonder Woman","y":"2009","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"CSM calls it an \"engaging animated superheroine tale\" that also \"has graphic violence.\" Fighting escalates to beatings, stabbings, and decapitations, with blood shown during an occult human sacrifice and a gruesome zombie onslaught; there are also cleavage-baring outfits, brief hair-covered topless bathing scenes, and references to prostitution and pick-up lines. Positive: the film champions strong, independent womanhood with Wonder Woman as a genuinely heroic role model, and her male love interest grows out of womanizing behavior over the course of the story.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/wonder-woman-2009","genre":["Animation","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":713,"t":"X-Men Origins: Wolverine","y":"2009","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"CSM recommends this darker franchise entry for age 13+, flagging frequent, brutal comic-book violence -- shootings, stabbings, claw-slashing, and grim medical-experimentation scenes -- plus occasional strong language (\"s--t\"), a couple making out, brief male nudity, and characters drinking beer and hard liquor. Positive: Logan grows into a leader, and the story argues mercy beats vengeance even if that message gets crowded out by nonstop action.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/x-men-origins-wolverine","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":714,"t":"Batman: Under the Red Hood","y":"2010","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"\"Drug dealers and brutal beatings in animated Batman movie.\" Concerns: brutal, bloody violence including the Joker beating Robin with a crowbar, gunfire, explosions, and character deaths, plus scattered language (\"hell,\" \"damn,\" \"scumbags\") and drug dealers in the plot. Positive: Batman holds to his no-killing code even as the film wrestles with morally murky ideas about justice and revenge.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-under-the-red-hood","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":715,"t":"Iron Man 2","y":"2010","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Downey whips out the big guns, sexy banter in fun sequel,\" says CSM. The sequel piles on explosions and two hanging deaths (CSM flags this as the film's worst moment), along with mild profanity (\"damn,\" \"bitches\"), a drunk Tony at his own birthday party, Ivan's frequent vodka drinking, and abundant sexual innuendo alongside skimpy costumes. Positive: the film argues that caring only about yourself isn't enough even facing death, with Pepper Potts' honesty and Rhodey's willingness to challenge Tony for the greater good standing out as the story's real role models.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/iron-man-2","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":716,"t":"Jonah Hex","y":"2010","la":"15+","ca":"15+","full":"CSM's tagline says it all: \"dumb, violent action Western totally misfires.\" Violence is intense and constant -- characters are shot, stabbed, chopped, and burned with a high body count -- alongside supernatural scares, mild sexual innuendo, and a female character depicted as a prostitute in skimpy lingerie. CSM notes positive messages and role models are very limited here: Hex is driven by revenge and kills without remorse, though the female lead does show some self-defense capability.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/jonah-hex","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Western"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":717,"t":"Justice League: Crisis on Two Earths","y":"2010","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"CSM describes it as a movie that \"mixes cartoon violence, positive messages.\" Expect extensive fighting with punches, arrows, and laser blasts, some blood, minor character deaths, and mild language like \"ass\" and \"crap.\" Positive: the film argues that giving in to bullies only empowers them, with Batman growing from refusing to help into embracing his responsibility, and Wonder Woman and Superman both modeling the courage it takes to stand up for what's right.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/justice-league-crisis-on-two-earths","genre":["Animation","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":718,"t":"Superman/Batman: Apocalypse","y":"2010","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Supergirl comes of age in so-so animated adventure.\" Concerns: superhero battles with heavy destruction and some blood (weapons, stabbing, a teen witnessing a parent's death), brief nude silhouettes, skimpy costumes, and mild language (\"ass,\" \"hell\"). Positive: Kara starts out dazzled by an over-the-top shopping montage but ultimately learns to stand up for herself and choose a life of responsibility over letting others control her fate.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/supermanbatman-apocalypse","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":719,"t":"All-Star Superman","y":"2011","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Violence and minor sensuality in reflective Superman tale.\" Facing his own impending death, Superman deals with several scary/intense superhero battles, a steamy shower scene, and mild language (\"hell,\" one \"son of a bitch\"). Positive: Superman responds to mortality with love, forgiveness, and kindness, modeling a gentler, more compassionate hero focused on helping others.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/all-star-superman","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":720,"t":"Batman: Year One","y":"2011","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"\"Animated superhero drama is too intense for young fans.\" Concerns: a kidnapped infant in mortal danger, bloody shootouts and a burning victim, red-light-district scenes with suggested prostitution, an extramarital affair, and language (\"hell,\" \"bitch,\" \"bastard,\" \"goddamn\"). Positive: Jim Gordon risks his career and safety to fight a corrupt police department, standing up for justice even as the film shows him making messy personal mistakes.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-year-one","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":721,"t":"Captain America: The First Avenger","y":"2011","la":"12+","ca":"11+","full":"\"Comic book adventure mixes patriotism, explosive action,\" says CSM. Gun battles, fireballs, and fistfights run throughout, including a point-blank shooting and a character pulled through an airplane propeller, plus a villain with an exposed skull-like face that can startle younger viewers, occasional \"hell\" and \"damn,\" pub drinking, and a couple of romantic kisses. Positive: CSM praises Steve Rogers as an old-fashioned, nearly flawless role model -- brave, compassionate, and self-sacrificing -- whose story champions courage and standing up for others over physical strength alone.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/captain-america-the-first-avenger","genre":["Live-Action","War","Superhero"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":722,"t":"Green Lantern","y":"2011","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"CSM describes it as an \"entertaining but superficial superhero thriller.\" The villain Parallax is genuinely frightening for younger kids, and there's a disturbing syringe-to-eye moment along with loud explosions and battle scenes; language includes occasional \"s--t,\" \"a--hole,\" and \"goddammit.\" Positive: the film's core message is that admitting fear is the first step to overcoming it, and cocky test pilot Hal Jordan proves to have a genuinely caring heart beneath the swagger, using his new powers for good.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/green-lantern","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":723,"t":"Green Lantern: Emerald Knights","y":"2011","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Violent, animated superhero movie with thoughtful messages.\" Concerns: near-constant fighting via power rings and hand-to-hand combat, some blue alien blood, and mild language (\"damn,\" \"hell\"). Positive: the anthology structure explores destiny versus free will, and new Green Lantern recruits show real courage and camaraderie despite veterans' occasional dismissiveness.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/green-lantern-emerald-knights","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":724,"t":"Thor","y":"2011","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Surprisingly complex superhero action-adventure,\" per CSM. Thor's battles bring stabbing, hitting, and explosions that stay intense without turning gory, plus mead-hall ale drinking at Asgardian celebrations, a little mild language (\"damn,\" \"hell\"), and a few kisses and comments about characters' looks. Positive: CSM highlights the film's message that revenge only spreads harm, embodied by Odin's effort to raise a son who tempers strength with wisdom, alongside a female scientist who won't compromise her research for anyone.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/thor","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Superhero"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":725,"t":"X-Men: First Class","y":"2011","la":"13+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Superhero film has action, thrills, tough moral questions.\" Set against the Cold War, the film includes major explosions, a boy watching his mother killed, and a coin turned lethal weapon, plus one use of \"f--k,\" 1960s-style social drinking and smoking, and Bond-style outfits implying nudity. Positive: Xavier's clear moral compass and Erik's more conflicted, revenge-driven arc give kids real material for discussing self-acceptance and fear of the unknown.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/x-men-first-class","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":726,"t":"Batman: The Dark Knight Returns Part 1","y":"2012","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"\"Extremely violent animated addition to the Batman story.\" Concerns: graphic fight scenes with stabbing and eye-gouging, a knife gang terrorizing citizens, a bloody flashback of the Waynes' murder, whiskey drinking, and mild language (\"damn,\" \"hell,\" \"son of a bitch\"). Positive: the film is less about clean heroism than about wrestling with good and evil in every person, as an aging Bruce Wayne returns to the cowl out of obligation rather than glory.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-the-dark-knight-returns-part-1","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":727,"t":"Ghost Rider: Spirit of Vengeance","y":"2012","la":"15+","ca":"15+","full":"Common Sense Media calls this sequel a \"crazy, violent, dumb action sequel [that] quickly burns out,\" rating it 15+. Characters burn and decay on-screen, a mother and young boy are slapped around, and the fights add gunfire and explosions to the skull-faced hero's usual mayhem; language includes one F-word plus \"d--k\" and \"goddamn,\" and a supporting character is referenced as an alcoholic. Positive: the film gestures at \"with great power comes great responsibility,\" though Johnny's devil's-bargain backstory undercuts it as real role-model material.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ghost-rider-spirit-of-vengeance","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":728,"t":"Justice League: Doom","y":"2012","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Great teamwork in otherwise violent superhero adventure.\" Concerns: heavy fist fighting and fantasy-weapon violence, characters set on fire, buried alive, and shot with visible blood, plus mild language (\"crap,\" \"damn,\" \"hell\"). Positive: seven heroes each contribute their own strengths to overcome the villains' plot, a strong lesson in teamwork even though Batman's short temper is on display.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/justice-league-doom","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":729,"t":"Superman vs. The Elite","y":"2012","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"\"Strong violence countered by positive messages, good story.\" Concerns: heavy comic-book violence (punching, battering, wartime destruction, and torture flashbacks), British-slang profanity plus \"ass,\" \"damn,\" and stronger words, frequent sexual innuendo from one character pursuing Superman, and a supporting character's drinking problem. Positive: Superman refuses to abandon his commitment to fairness and non-lethal justice even under public pressure to get tougher, and Lois Lane is shown as brave and sharp.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/superman-vs-the-elite","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":730,"t":"The Avengers","y":"2012","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Heroes work together in explosive comic-book adventure,\" per CSM. Massive battle sequences level city blocks with implied heavy casualties, alien combat, and weapons fire throughout, plus profanity like \"son of a bitch,\" \"bastard,\" and \"hell,\" minimal drinking, and only flirty banter for romance. Positive: CSM emphasizes teamwork as the throughline -- each hero works through a personal flaw, from Iron Man's selfishness to Black Widow and Hawkeye's troubled pasts -- to unite around a shared mission.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-avengers","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":731,"t":"The Dark Knight Rises","y":"2012","la":"15+","ca":"15+","full":"\"Bale's final Batman is as gritty and dark as the first two,\" per Common Sense Media. The trilogy closer delivers frequent, cringe-inducingly realistic violence, including neck-breakings, stabbings, and mass explosions with a high body count; language stays fairly sparse (\"bitch,\" \"damn,\" \"hell\"), cocktails appear at formal events, and Bruce Wayne shares passionate kisses with two women, one leading to an off-camera night together shown afterward in bare shoulders and embraces. Positive: the film explores identity, responsibility, sacrifice, and justice, with Batman modeling courage and selflessness while supporting characters John Blake and Selina Kyle show loyalty and growth.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-dark-knight-rises","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":732,"t":"Batman: The Dark Knight Returns Part 2","y":"2013","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"\"Extremely violent, ambiguous end to animated Batman saga.\" Concerns: near-constant fighting with guns, stabbing, and visible blood, an escort-service subplot, partial nudity under Nazi-style imagery, and characters drinking to cope. Positive: Batman rallies a scattered group toward a greater good, though the film leaves viewers to wrestle with whether vigilante justice is really justice at all.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-the-dark-knight-returns-part-2","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":733,"t":"Iron Man 3","y":"2013","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Fewer playboy antics, but still plenty of violence,\" says CSM. Public bombings and assassinations bring a more grounded, human-scaled violence than earlier films, with language including \"s--t,\" \"hell,\" and \"goddamn,\" party and bar drinking (with one character admitting past substance problems), and women shown in bikinis and lingerie. Positive: CSM points to Tony Stark's real growth here -- toward commitment, selflessness, and mentoring a young boy -- as a caution that unchecked power breeds hubris.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/iron-man-3","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":734,"t":"Justice League: The Flashpoint Paradox","y":"2013","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Violent animation about tragic pasts has frenzied action.\" Concerns: intense violence including Wonder Woman holding a severed head and causing a death by hanging, a bloodied face after a beating, a child discovering his murdered mother, a flask-drinking parent, and scattered profanity (\"hell,\" \"crap,\" \"a--hole\"). Positive: the story turns on themes of accepting what can't be changed (echoing the Serenity Prayer) as Flash reckons with grief over his mother's death.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/justice-league-flashpoint-paradox","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":735,"t":"Man of Steel","y":"2013","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"CSM calls it a \"brooding, action-packed reboot\" for Superman, recommending age 12+. Destruction is constant - buildings collapse, cars flatten, aircraft go down - and the climactic fight includes Superman breaking a villain's neck, a first for the character; language stays occasional (\"s--t,\" one use of \"f-ing\"). Positive: both of Clark's fathers model responsibility and selfless guidance, and Superman himself consistently uses his power to protect others rather than himself.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/man-of-steel","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":736,"t":"Superman: Unbound","y":"2013","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"\"Superman fights, learns life lessons in animated thriller.\" Concerns: heavy super-powered fighting, terrorist threats with guns, bombs, and missiles, mild language (\"hell,\" \"God\"), and a grown-up romance between Superman and Lois Lane with kissing. Positive: Superman remains a steady role model for kids who feel like outsiders, and both he and Supergirl learn that overprotecting the people you love keeps everyone from truly living.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/superman-unbound","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":737,"t":"The Wolverine","y":"2013","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"\"Thrilling, action-packed adventure treads dark waters.\" Wolverine's claws maim and kill across bloody, bone-crunching fights, guns and swords are everywhere, and language includes \"bitch,\" \"s--t,\" and one \"f--k\"; there's implied intercourse and a scene of three underwear-clad characters kissing, plus some bar drinking. Positive: Logan's fierce sense of justice, and Yukio and Mariko's proactive strength -- they rescue him as often as he rescues them.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-wolverine","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":738,"t":"Thor: The Dark World","y":"2013","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Teen-friendly comic-book sequel is brutal but not gory,\" per CSM. Knife fights, gunfights, and explosions drive the action, with a single use of \"s--t\" among milder language, some social drinking, kissing and flirting, and one brief non-sexual scene of blurred male nudity. Positive: CSM highlights the film's takeaway that it's better to be a good man than a king, with Thor's steadfast convictions and the story's openness to redemption for even compromised characters.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/thor-the-dark-world","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Superhero"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":739,"t":"Batman: Assault on Arkham","y":"2014","la":"14+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Cynical and violent superhero tale has some cursing,\" says Common Sense Media. Amanda Waller sends the Suicide Squad -- Harley Quinn, Deadshot, Killer Frost -- into Arkham Asylum, and the film delivers exploding and frozen-then-shattered heads, hangings, and regular profanity (\"s--t,\" \"a--hole\"). Harley Quinn is shown undressing and two characters are implied to have sex, plus the Joker jokes about abusing her. Positive messages are essentially absent; CSM notes the story centers Batman's villains rather than any real role models, making it best suited for older teen superfans.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-assault-on-arkham","genre":["Animation","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":740,"t":"Captain America: The Winter Soldier","y":"2014","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Heavy fantasy violence, but no sex in superhero sequel,\" says CSM. Frequent hand-to-hand combat, shooting, and falls bring some blood and explosions, with \"s--t\" occasionally audible under the battle noise, minimal champagne drinking, and only a strategic kiss between Cap and Black Widow used to dodge pursuers. Positive: CSM praises the film's stand that \"fear does not equal freedom,\" with Cap modeling courage, integrity, and loyalty while the story questions weapons stockpiling and preemptive strikes.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/captain-america-the-winter-soldier","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":741,"t":"Guardians of the Galaxy","y":"2014","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Marvel sci-fi adventure has laughs and action violence,\" per CSM. Fistfights, shootouts, and explosions run through the film alongside a handful of character deaths, strong language including \"s--t\" and \"bulls--t,\" sexual tension and references to Peter Quill's past conquests, and unnamed alcohol leading to onscreen drunkenness. Positive: CSM says the flawed Guardians ultimately choose teamwork and selflessness over self-interest, protecting each other even at personal cost.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/guardians-of-the-galaxy","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Superhero"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":742,"t":"Justice League: War","y":"2014","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Dark animated tale has constant violence, some profanity.\" Concerns: unrelenting comic-book violence with sword fights and characters thrown through buildings, bullying and taunting between heroes, and language including \"s--t,\" \"son of a bitch,\" and \"ass.\" Positive: despite early infighting and disrespect toward Wonder Woman, the team ultimately learns the value of teamwork and perseverance against a shared threat.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/justice-league-war","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":743,"t":"Son of Batman","y":"2014","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Violent and noir-ish Batman is engaging but cynical,\" per Common Sense Media. Bruce Wayne discovers he has a hot-tempered son, Damian, and the pair face frequent sword-and-gun combat, including eye stabbings and a child pinned to a wall by blades. There's mild profanity (\"bastard,\" \"screw you\") and passing sex references, like a villain keeping prostitutes at his apartment. Positive: the film explores a complicated father-son bond, though CSM notes there isn't much traditional heroism -- the characters are deeply flawed rather than role models.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/dcu-son-of-batman","genre":["Animation","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":744,"t":"X-Men: Days of Future Past","y":"2014","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Time-traveling superhero sequel has lots of action violence.\" Bone-crunching super-powered battles include stabbing, shooting, and visible corpses; language includes \"s--t\" and \"a--hole\"; young Xavier injects a mobility-restoring serum styled like drug use and drinks heavily early on; a mutant's blue form appears unclothed. Positive: the film argues killing never solves anything -- friendship, unity, and hope do -- with Xavier modeling generosity and ethical leadership.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/x-men-days-of-future-past","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Sci-Fi","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":745,"t":"Ant-Man","y":"2015","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Clever, funny, cool Marvel movie has lots of sci-fi action,\" per CSM. Fistfights and shootouts include villains dissolving into \"gelatinous goo,\" scattered strong language (\"s--t,\" \"ass,\" \"damn\"), a couple of suggestive jokes and one kissing scene, and social champagne, beer, and wine drinking without excess. Positive: CSM highlights forgiveness and redemption as the film's core themes, with Scott Lang and Hank Pym both working to protect their daughters and Hope van Dyne portrayed as a strong, capable partner rather than a sidekick.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ant-man","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Superhero"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":746,"t":"Avengers: Age of Ultron","y":"2015","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Superheroes reteam for entertaining, action-packed sequel,\" says CSM. The nonstop battling stays cartoonish rather than graphic but implies heavy civilian casualties throughout, with profanity like \"son-of-a-bitch\" and \"bastard,\" some celebratory drinking, and kissing and flirting in form-fitting costumes. Positive: CSM frames the team's motto -- \"united you stand, divided you fall\" -- as the film's real lesson, though it also notes both female heroes need rescuing at points, a wrinkle worth discussing.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/avengers-age-of-ultron","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":747,"t":"Batman vs. Robin","y":"2015","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Excellent tale with disturbing imagery, frequent violence,\" says Common Sense Media. Ten-year-old Damian/Robin rebels against his father's \"justice, not vengeance\" code, and the story includes a child abuser called the Dollmaker who sews doll masks onto children's faces, flashbacks to young Bruce watching his parents' murder, and a scene of Batman hallucinating after ingesting a drug. There's mild profanity, gun violence, and an implied-sex scene between Bruce and his girlfriend. Positive: CSM praises the film's honest look at Batman and Damian as \"cold, damaged, and temperamental\" people striving toward the film's core lesson about justice over vengeance.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-vs-robin","genre":["Animation","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":748,"t":"Fantastic Four","y":"2015","la":"13+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Disappointing adventure wastes talented cast, gets bloody.\" This darker reboot has the villain killing dozens of people, sometimes graphically (blood, an exploding head), and The Thing racking up a body count as a military weapon, plus profanity (\"s--t,\" \"ass,\" \"d--k,\" \"damn\") and young characters drinking and making poor choices as a result. Positive: despite the grim tone, the four leads pull together as a team and the film argues young people deserve to be trusted with real responsibility.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/fantastic-four-2015","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Sci-Fi","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":749,"t":"Justice League: Gods and Monsters","y":"2015","la":"14+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Engaging noir tale is dark and violent; some cursing, nudity,\" per Common Sense Media. This alternate-universe Justice League story includes a sword-to-the-chest killing, gunfire, explosions, and demonic imagery, plus profanity (\"s--t,\" \"goddammit\") and nudity -- a female android appears fully nude, and Wonder Woman wears revealing clothing. A college flashback references marijuana use. Positive: the film wrestles seriously with justice, power, and whether the ends justify the means, per CSM, even as its heroes remain morally complicated rather than idealized.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/justice-league-gods-and-monsters","genre":["Animation","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":750,"t":"Justice League: Throne of Atlantis","y":"2015","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Animated Aquaman origin story has violence, cursing,\" per Common Sense Media. Arthur Curry, grieving his father, gets severely drunk in a Maine dive bar and brawls with a group of longshoremen before later facing stabbings, a shark-attack death, and demonic imagery from an Atlantean uprising. Language includes \"s--t,\" \"crap,\" and \"bastard.\" Positive: the film is honest about grief and imperfection -- Superman and Wonder Woman struggle with their own powers -- though CSM found few clear positive messages in this darker take on the team's formation.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/justice-league-throne-of-atlantis","genre":["Animation","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":751,"t":"Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice","y":"2016","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"Common Sense Media describes it as a \"promising but overlong superhero mash-up [that] is dark, violent,\" with an age recommendation of 13+. The body count is high from assassinations, building collapses, and an extended Batman-Superman brawl; a bathtub scene between Clark and Lois shows her bare shoulders and legs, and there's some partial nudity elsewhere. Positive: despite their early rivalry, Batman and Superman ultimately choose teamwork over conflict, and Wonder Woman's introduction offers an independent, agency-driven female hero.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-v-superman-dawn-of-justice","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":752,"t":"Batman: Bad Blood","y":"2016","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"\"Superviolent superhero mayhem with a complex story,\" per Common Sense Media. With Batman missing, Nightwing, Robin, Batwing, and the newly introduced Batwoman fight a consortium of villains through buildings exploding and collapsing, gunfights, swordplay, and characters who appear dead before reviving. Mild profanity (\"crap,\" \"damn,\" \"bitch\") runs throughout, and a brief fantasy sequence shows Batman partially nude. Positive: CSM highlights an ethnically diverse team modeling intelligence, bravery, and loyalty, with the message that \"good triumphs over evil\" through teamwork -- including Batwoman's canon debut as a lesbian character.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-bad-blood","genre":["Animation","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":753,"t":"Captain America: Civil War","y":"2016","la":"12+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Intense, entertaining adventure is a great threequel.\" The Avengers split into warring factions, with tons of explosions, collateral damage, and even a graphically depicted flashback to a past violent act. Language includes scattered \"s--t\" and \"son of a bitch,\" plus a quick kiss between Cap and a love interest. Positive: the film wrestles with loyalty, personal will versus collective responsibility, and honesty, with most Avengers showing genuine remorse over civilian casualties.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/captain-america-civil-war","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":754,"t":"Doctor Strange","y":"2016","la":"11+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Mysticism, humor, and action surround unique Marvel hero.\" Expect large-scale magical destruction, a brutal car crash, a beheading (no gore shown), and unsettling operating-room scenes with blood. Language is fairly mild, and there's brief reference to a past relationship. Positive: arrogant surgeon Stephen Strange learns that \"it's not about you,\" embracing humility, perseverance, and eventual self-sacrifice for the greater good.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/doctor-strange","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Fantasy"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":755,"t":"Justice League vs. Teen Titans","y":"2016","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Animated violence, demonic imagery in solid DC installment,\" says Common Sense Media. Batman sends his son Damian to train with the Teen Titans just as the demon Trigon possesses the Justice League, leading to sword decapitation, electrocution, and explosions, plus mild language (\"hell,\" \"damn\") and Starfire shown in a skimpy, revealing robe on a video call. Positive: CSM notes Damian starts out arrogant and self-centered but grows through the story, ultimately learning to value teamwork and family alongside his new teammates.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/justice-league-vs-teen-titans","genre":["Animation","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":756,"t":"Suicide Squad","y":"2016","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"CSM's tagline calls it a \"muddled, violent mess\" where \"DC's antiheroes disappoint,\" rating it 14+. Assassinations and shootouts run throughout, language includes \"s--t,\" \"p---y,\" and sexist dialogue about controlling women, and one scene shows a man striking a woman. Positive: the \"honor among thieves\" arc gives hardened criminals like Deadshot and Diablo real moments of parental love and redemption, per CSM's review.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/suicide-squad","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":757,"t":"X-Men: Apocalypse","y":"2016","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Superhero-size violence, fab effects in '80s-set threequel.\" Expect big explosions, hand-to-hand fighting, a bloody Wolverine rampage, and a tragic child death, plus one strong swear and milder language (\"damn,\" \"hell,\" \"ass\"); a few characters wear skimpy, armor-like outfits. Positive: \"united you stand, divided you crumble\" -- Professor X's faith in people and Mystique's loyalty anchor a message about teamwork beating fear.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/x-men-apocalypse","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Sci-Fi","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":758,"t":"Batman and Harley Quinn","y":"2017","la":"14+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Noir superhero story has sex, cursing, and violence,\" says Common Sense Media, warning that this entry skews more adult than typical Batman DTV films. Batman and Nightwing reluctantly team with a paroled Harley Quinn against ecoterrorist villains Poison Ivy and Jason Woodrue, in scenes featuring monster-stabbing deaths, martial arts combat, occasional profanity, and more sexual content than usual -- including implied sex and crude humor around Harley. Positive: CSM notes the film's underlying message that doing wrong to fight wrongdoing is still wrong, even as its cynical, cursing heroes fall short of being traditional role models.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-harley-quinn","genre":["Animation","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":759,"t":"Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2","y":"2017","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Lots of laughs, thrills, tunes in slightly lesser sequel.\" Frequent weapon battles and space combat include people being \"spaced\" out of airlocks, high body counts, and a major character's death. Language is stronger than the original (\"s--t,\" \"bulls--t,\" \"d--k\"), and Ravagers drink to the point of drunkenness at a pub. Positive: the flawed, roguish Guardians make selfless choices for one another, driving home themes of teamwork, redemption, and found family.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/guardians-of-the-galaxy-vol-2","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":760,"t":"Justice League","y":"2017","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"All-star superhero adventure is uneven but entertaining.\" Concerns: large-scale battle violence with explosions, destruction, and hand-to-hand combat; scattered profanity (\"s--t,\" \"a--hole,\" \"son of a bitch\"); kissing between reunited lovers; and Aquaman downing a bottle of liquor before tossing it into the sea. Positive: the film centers on teamwork and working with others for the greater good, with Batman in particular learning the value of collaboration over solo heroics.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/justice-league","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":761,"t":"Teen Titans: The Judas Contract","y":"2017","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Superteens resist betrayal and brutality; violence, cursing,\" per Common Sense Media. Cult leader Brother Blood hires the mercenary Deathstroke to hunt the Titans one by one, using an inside traitor, in a story full of point-blank shootings, torture, lasers, and hand-to-hand combat, plus occasional profanity (\"s--t,\" \"piss off\") and some kissing and revealing costumes. Positive: CSM praises the film's message that \"good defeats evil\" and its redefinition of family as loyal, unselfish people who work together -- including a betrayer's arc toward making amends.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/teen-titans-the-judas-contract","genre":["Animation","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":762,"t":"The Lego Batman Movie","y":"2017","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"\"Clever, creative, action-packed adventure/toy ad.\" Darker and more explosive than the first Lego Movie, with constant battles, bombs, and destruction (all bloodless, since everyone's plastic), plus potty-adjacent insults like \"stupid\" and \"sucks,\" a joke about Robin going pantsless, and Alfred sipping wine. Positive: Batman's arc from egotistical loner to team player anchors the film, and Barbara Gordon is written as sharp and capable, with the story's real lesson being that working as a team with people you care about beats doing everything yourself.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-lego-batman-movie","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":763,"t":"Thor: Ragnarok","y":"2017","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Funniest Thor movie has tons of style, plenty of violence.\" Sword-and-sorcery battles include a bloody stabbing of a main character and a melting death, plus frequent explosions; numerous characters die. Language includes \"s--t\" and \"hell\" alongside adolescent humor, and one character has long used alcohol to cope with trauma. Positive: Thor grows from arrogant prince into a worthy, humble leader, while Valkyrie models courage in the face of past trauma, with teamwork as a throughline.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/thor-ragnarok","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Comedy"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":764,"t":"Wonder Woman","y":"2017","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Engaging, entertaining, empowering superhero adventure,\" per Common Sense Media, which recommends age 12+. Frequent WWI battle violence brings a real body count, a naked man briefly covers himself in one scene, and adults drink at pubs and receptions with one character shown intoxicated. Positive: Diana models courage, selflessness, and compassion as a genuine equal to her male co-stars, and CSM praises the film's message that women can be just as capable warriors and leaders as men.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/wonder-woman","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":765,"t":"Ant-Man and the Wasp","y":"2018","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Funny, action-packed sequel is lighter Marvel fare.\" Frequent sci-fi chases and hand-to-hand combat put major characters in peril, including flashbacks of children in distress, though no major characters die; one character is drugged without consent. Language includes infrequent \"s--t\" and \"ass,\" and adults drink wine with meals. Positive: the film promotes forgiveness, redemption, and teamwork, with Hope van Dyne -- Marvel's first female hero with title billing -- shown as strong and capable alongside Scott's devotion to his daughter.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ant-man-and-the-wasp","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Comedy"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":766,"t":"Aquaman","y":"2018","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"\"Underwater origin story has lots of comic book action,\" per CSM. Concerns: extensive fantasy fighting with impalements, drownings, and monster attacks (largely bloodless despite the intensity); scattered profanity (\"s--t,\" \"ass,\" \"d--k\"); adult kissing and a shirtless Aquaman; and a bar scene where a character gets drunk with little consequence. Positive: courage, loyalty, and accepting responsibility for the greater good, with Mera and Atlanna written as capable characters rather than damsels in distress.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/aquaman","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":767,"t":"Avengers: Infinity War","y":"2018","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Intense violence, strong messages in shocking Marvel epic.\" This is the darkest MCU entry yet, with frequent, massively destructive action including brutal beatings, impalings, and stabbings, plus real, lasting consequences for beloved characters. Language includes a couple uses of \"s--t\" and two aborted f-word attempts, and one scene implies a couple has been intimate. Positive: heroism, self-sacrifice, and teamwork drive the story, with a diverse cast of heroes -- including strong women and a resourceful teenage Spider-Man -- fighting with equal bravery.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/avengers-infinity-war","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":768,"t":"Batman Ninja","y":"2018","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Anime Batman time-travels to ancient Japan; lots of action.\" Gorilla Grodd's time machine strands Batman and a roster of DC heroes and villains in feudal Japan for nonstop sword, spear, and cannon battles, plus mild swearing (\"hell,\" \"damn\") and some female characters drawn in revealing outfits with light innuendo. Positive: Batman's courage, intelligence, and willingness to sacrifice for others carry through no matter what century he's in, reinforcing that good wins out over evil.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-ninja","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":769,"t":"Black Panther","y":"2018","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Masterful Marvel film has depth, diversity -- and violence.\" Close-up ritual combat includes bloody wounds, stabbings, and spears and curved knives in play, with more widespread explosive violence than earlier Marvel films. Language is infrequent (\"s--t,\" \"ass,\" \"hell\"), and adults drink socially in the background. Positive: the film promotes teamwork, integrity, and courage, with T'Challa's thoughtful leadership and strong, capable women like Okoye, Nakia, and tech genius Shuri offering standout role models.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/black-panther","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":770,"t":"Teen Titans Go! To the Movies","y":"2018","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"\"Irreverent, immature comedy cleverly mocks superhero genre.\" Expect nonstop cartoonish violence with guns, missiles, and swords, plenty of toilet humor (farts, poop, \"diarrhea\"), mild insults like \"morons\" and \"losers,\" and a couple of brief animated bare bottoms. Positive: the Titans' female members are shown as every bit as capable as the boys, and the film's central message -- that friendship matters more than fame or box-office success -- comes through even when the team's problem-solving leans on chaos over caution.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/teen-titans-go-to-the-movies","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":771,"t":"Venom","y":"2018","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"\"Violent, uninspired comic book tale misses the mark,\" says Common Sense Media, which sets the age recommendation at 14+. Expect intense fantasy violence with slicing, stabbing, and jump-scare fangs from Venom himself, plus strong language (\"s--t,\" one F-word, one use of \"p---y\") and an implied-sex scene between Eddie and Anne. Positive: the film's message that \"everyone has a right to live, human or not\" comes through Anne's forgiveness toward even the most monstrous characters.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/venom","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Sony Pictures","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":772,"t":"Avengers: Endgame","y":"2019","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Intense but satisfying finale is an epic gift to MCU fans.\" Frequent, intense comic-book violence includes dismemberment, decapitation, stabbing, and destructive explosions, and several beloved characters die in ways that may upset viewers. Language includes \"son of a bitch\" and \"ass\" (even from Captain America), and Thor's heavy drinking as a grief coping mechanism is a recurring subplot. Positive: courage, self-sacrifice, and the idea that \"family is more than pure biology\" run throughout, as flawed heroes become stronger together and choose the collective good.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/avengers-endgame","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":773,"t":"Batman: Hush","y":"2019","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"Batman teams up with Catwoman to unravel the identity of Hush, a mysterious new villain who knows all his secrets. Common Sense Media notes cartoon violence throughout -- deaths of major characters, explosions, brutal hand-to-hand fights, gunfire, and a decapitation -- plus language like \"s--t,\" \"bitch,\" and \"goddamn.\" Batman and Catwoman's romance includes kissing and an implied night together, and characters drink socially. Positive: Batman lives by a strict moral code, refusing to kill even a defeated enemy, and the story rewards loyalty and resourcefulness.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-hush","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":774,"t":"Captain Marvel","y":"2019","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"\"Powerful female superhero story has some violence, language.\" Combat mixes fistfights with fantasy weapons like laser scepters and photon blasts, plus explosions and minimal alien blood; all on-screen deaths are extraterrestrial. Language stays fairly mild (\"s--t,\" \"hell,\" \"ass\"), with no real sex or substance content. Positive: Carol Danvers is a fearless, capable lead who is never objectified, and the film's \"you're stronger than you think\" message is reinforced by courageous supporting women like Maria Rambeau and Dr. Wendy Lawson.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/captain-marvel","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":776,"t":"Dark Phoenix","y":"2019","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Sophie Turner is best part of intense, dark X-Men finale.\" The trilogy-ender features heavy superpower battles, impalement, alien attacks, and an upsetting death, plus profanity (\"s--t,\" \"damn,\" one \"f---ing\") and party drinking. Positive: the story favors teamwork over vengeance, with Jean's inner conflict, Scott's protectiveness, and Xavier's continued advocacy for mutant acceptance giving the ensemble real emotional weight.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/dark-phoenix","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Sci-Fi","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":777,"t":"Justice League vs. the Fatal Five","y":"2019","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Superheroes encounter brutal villains from future; violence,\" per Common Sense Media. Three members of the 31st-century Fatal Five hijack a time-travel capsule to hunt young Green Lantern Jessica Cruz, unleashing continuous explosions, fireballs, gunfire, and swordplay, plus mild profanity (\"hell,\" \"bastard\") and one scene of a character running naked through the streets. Positive: CSM praises the film for portraying Jessica's PTSD and another hero's paranoia with care, showing characters with mental health struggles working toward recovery while the team proves strong, loyal, and brave.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/justice-league-vs-the-fatal-five","genre":["Animation","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":778,"t":"Reign of the Supermen","y":"2019","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Animated sequel with frequent violence and a complex plot,\" per Common Sense Media. Six months after Superman's apparent death, four new \"Supermen\" emerge in Metropolis while Lois Lane investigates, amid explosions, gunfire, alien attacks, and brutal hand-to-hand combat, plus mild profanity (\"hell,\" \"son of a bitch\") and Lois using seduction to gather information. Positive: CSM highlights Lois as resourceful and strong and the Justice League as determined and loyal, with the story affirming that good triumphs over evil and that heroic power shouldn't be underestimated.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/reign-of-the-supermen","genre":["Animation","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":779,"t":"Shazam!","y":"2019","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Superhero comedy is charming, with some scary/violent parts.\" Concerns: car crashes, gunfire, and disintegrating characters, plus the monstrous Seven Deadly Sins killing people in disturbing ways; moderate profanity (\"s--t,\" \"ass,\" \"hell\"); two visits to a gentlemen's club played for comedy; and characters buying beer before switching drinks. Positive: the loving Vasquez foster family models found family and support, and Billy learns to move past selfishness toward gratitude and teamwork.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/shazam","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Comedy"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":780,"t":"Wonder Woman: Bloodlines","y":"2019","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"This animated origin story sends Wonder Woman from Themyscira into the human world to protect a troubled teenage girl from a criminal syndicate. Common Sense Media flags frequent violence -- bloody deaths, explosions, gunfire, and lengthy hand-to-hand combat -- along with language such as \"bitch\" and \"s--t,\" a kiss, and some revealing costumes. Positive: the nearly all-female cast shows women as courageous, smart game-changers, and the film models that doing right sometimes means taking risks.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/wonder-woman-bloodlines","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":781,"t":"Superman: Man of Tomorrow","y":"2020","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Graphic violence and imagery in animated origin story,\" per Common Sense Media, this follows a young Clark Kent interning at the Daily Planet while learning to become the Man of Steel. The review flags lengthy fights with firearms, flame, and chains leaving characters bloody and beaten, profanity like \"s--t\" and \"a--holes,\" Superman briefly losing his clothes to flames, and the alien Lobo binge-drinking from a bottle. Positive: the film promotes diversity, though CSM notes even Lois Lane turns manipulative here.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/superman-man-of-tomorrow","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":782,"t":"Superman: Red Son","y":"2020","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"Reimagining Superman's rocket landing in Soviet Ukraine instead of Kansas, this alternate-history tale follows him growing up as a tool of Stalin's Cold War propaganda machine. Common Sense Media notes Superman kills Stalin with heat vision, Batman dies by suicide rather than return to the gulag, and there's schoolyard violence, profanity (\"s--t,\" \"bitch,\" \"hell\"), and Lois Lane shown as a heavy smoker trying to quit. Positive: strong, independent female characters and a thoughtful look at how power can be used or misused.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/superman-red-son","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":783,"t":"The New Mutants","y":"2020","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"\"Groundbreaking elements, but violent X-Men tale falls short.\" This horror-tinged spinoff includes stories of teens who've killed people (some remorseful, some not), creature attacks, torture scenes, and bloody imagery, plus strong profanity (\"a--hole,\" \"bulls--t,\" \"bitch\"), a bully's racist language, and both same-sex and opposite-sex teen make-out scenes with some shirtless/partial nudity. Positive: an unusually diverse cast across race, gender, and sexuality, with characters confronting fear and trauma through self-discovery.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-new-mutants","genre":["Live-Action","Horror","Superhero"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":784,"t":"Wonder Woman 1984","y":"2020","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Melancholy DC sequel about redemption has mixed messages.\" Concerns: frequent action violence, including gunplay and a scene where a character's appearance disturbingly deteriorates with blood dripping from his ears and nose; infrequent strong language and sexist catcalling; several passionate kisses and a scene implying a couple has slept together, shown in bed; and social drinking at a reception. Positive: women's skill as warriors and fighters, plus compassion and courage, though the film also suggests appearance affects women's self-worth.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/wonder-woman-1984","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":785,"t":"Batman: The Long Halloween, Part One","y":"2021","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Violence, smoking, language in noir DC action-mystery,\" says Common Sense Media of this adaptation, where Batman, Commissioner Gordon, and Harvey Dent hunt a holiday-themed killer. The review cites characters shot and killed with a silenced gun, a stabbing, a man pushed down a stairwell, cigarette and pipe smoking, and beer and wine drinking, with infrequent profanity (\"s--t,\" \"damn,\" \"hell\"). Positive: the film questions vigilante justice and values teamwork, even as CSM notes its heroes come across as flawed and human rather than simply good.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-the-long-halloween-part-one","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Mystery"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":786,"t":"Black Widow","y":"2021","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Strong women in ScarJo superhero film; violence, language.\" Extensive combat includes knives, punches, and a blunt instrument, plus explosions and a massive avalanche that put characters in peril. Language includes \"bitch,\" \"s--t,\" and \"dammit,\" and characters drink vodka and beer in moderation at gatherings. Positive: the film centers women's independence from those who'd control them, sisterhood, and found family, with Natasha, Yelena, and Melina shown as strong, clever, and powerful especially when they work together.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/black-widow","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":787,"t":"Eternals","y":"2021","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"\"Visually strong but talky MCU tale has violence, sex scene.\" Frequent action against aggressive, goopy-gore monsters is layered with heavier real-world imagery, including references to the bombing of Hiroshima, and main characters die. There's one love scene showing bare shoulders and backs with clearly implied sex, plus adults drinking beer and liquor at gatherings; language includes \"s--t\" and \"ass.\" Positive: sacrifice, unconditional love, and teamwork run through the story, with characters modeling selfless caregiving and compassion even as a few grapple with morally difficult choices.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/eternals","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":788,"t":"Justice Society: World War II","y":"2021","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"The Flash travels back in time and joins Wonder Woman's Justice Society to fight Nazis during World War II. Common Sense Media notes superhero and war violence throughout -- bombs, machine guns, explosions, and hand-to-hand combat -- plus cigarette smoking and a bottle of wine, with minimal language (\"hell\"). Positive: Wonder Woman emerges as the team's clear, capable leader, and the story emphasizes teamwork and standing against tyranny.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/justice-society-world-war-ii","genre":["Animation","Superhero","War"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":789,"t":"Shang-Chi and the Legend of the Ten Rings","y":"2021","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"\"Funny, poignant, thrilling MCU adventure; action violence.\" Frequent martial-arts combat features guns, spears, bows, explosions, deaths and injuries, plus scary demon-like monsters in the third act. Language stays occasional (\"s--t,\" \"damn,\" \"hell\"), with only light romantic tension between two leads. Positive: courage, heroism, and protecting those you love anchor the story, with Shang-Chi shown as brave, empathetic, and kind, and the film notable for its nearly all-Asian/Asian diaspora cast.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/shang-chi-and-the-legend-of-the-ten-rings","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":790,"t":"Venom: Let There Be Carnage","y":"2021","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"CSM describes this sequel as \"noisy violence and forced comedy in a poor super-sequel,\" recommending it for 14+. Carnage's rampage includes a head bitten off and eye-gouging, an animated flashback depicts child abuse, and language ramps up with an F-word and repeated \"s--t\"; a flashback also briefly shows cartoon nudity. Positive: Eddie and Venom's arc leans on teamwork, showing the pair (unlike their enemies) actually succeed once they stop fighting each other.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/venom-let-there-be-carnage","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Sony Pictures","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":791,"t":"Batman and Superman: Battle of the Super Sons","y":"2022","la":"11+","ca":"Not on CSM; substitute source used","full":"Not on Common Sense Media; per Wikipedia and IMDb's parents' guide, this animated adventure has young Jon Kent and Damian Wayne team up after the alien Starro infects the Justice League and Teen Titans with mind-controlling spores. IMDb notes moderate violence -- punches and kicks with visible blood, aliens bursting from mouths -- plus mild language (a couple uses of \"damn\") and no sex or substance content. Positive: the story centers on two young heroes learning to trust and work with each other despite their fathers' rivalry.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Batman_and_Superman:_Battle_of_the_Super_Sons","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":792,"t":"Black Adam","y":"2022","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Lots of bashing and smashing in disappointing DC movie,\" says CSM. Concerns: a high body count from electrocution, stabbing, shooting, and destruction, all gruesome but bloodless; sporadic profanity (\"s--t,\" \"bastard,\" \"damn,\" \"hell\"); and brief flirting. Positive: the heroes are brave and try to do what's right even when they make mistakes, and the story notably puts the fate of Kahndaq in the hands of its own people rather than outside rescuers.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/black-adam","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":793,"t":"Black Panther: Wakanda Forever","y":"2022","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Epic, women-led sequel is part tribute, part intense battle.\" The film opens with the off-screen death of King T'Challa and processes real grief before Wakanda faces the underwater nation of Talokan, with combat, drowning, and an emotional second death along the way. Positive: Queen Ramonda, Okoye, Nakia, and Shuri lead as strong, smart, and brave role models, and the story favors diplomacy and collaboration over revenge.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/black-panther-wakanda-forever","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":794,"t":"Catwoman: Hunted","y":"2022","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Female-centered superhero story has violence, some innuendo,\" per Common Sense Media, this anime-style adventure catches Catwoman between Interpol, a global crime syndicate, and Batwoman. The review notes fighting with assault weapons, swords, and steel claws, a character impaled on rebar, mild language (\"crap,\" \"ass,\" \"hell\"), and strong innuendo between Catwoman and Batwoman including a bathing scene. Positive: CSM highlights that the primary heroes and villain are all women in a traditionally male-dominated genre.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/catwoman-hunted","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":795,"t":"DC League of Super-Pets","y":"2022","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Furry cartoon superhero actioner is funny, cute; rude humor.\" Krypto and friends get into plenty of cartoon mayhem -- crashes, explosions, characters hit by cars -- plus bleeped-out profanity, a wisecracking turtle with adult-tinged innuendo, and a subplot involving lab-tested animals; the hero cast includes an engaged lesbian couple. Positive: the film's message that you'll do anything for someone you love, even let them go, comes through the pets' teamwork and self-sacrifice to save the people (and each other) they love.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/dc-league-of-super-pets","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Superhero","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":796,"t":"Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness","y":"2022","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Twisty, sometimes terrifying MCU film has scares, violence.\" Director Sam Raimi pushes the MCU into horror territory, with jump scares, a body-snatching villain, and graphic depictions of death, burning, and destruction; strong language includes \"s--t\" and \"goddamn,\" and adults drink martinis at a wedding. Positive: the film champions compassion, perseverance, and teamwork, with America Chavez showing real courage and Doctor Strange learning humility and the value of asking forgiveness.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/doctor-strange-in-the-multiverse-of-madness","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Fantasy"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":797,"t":"Morbius","y":"2022","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"CSM sums it up as \"fighting and biting in a by-the-numbers superhero movie,\" rated 14+. Dr. Morbius's vampire-bat serum leads to clawed, blood-spattered violence and a bullying scene where a child is struck with a crutch; language is milder (\"a few uses of s--t\"), and hypodermic needles appear as Morbius injects his formula. Positive: the film does wrestle, if unevenly, with whether Morbius's power is \"a gift or a curse\" rather than treating his new abilities as pure triumph.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/morbius","genre":["Live-Action","Horror","Superhero"],"studio":"Sony Pictures","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":798,"t":"The Batman","y":"2022","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"CSM calls it a \"confident and mature yet dark, violent Batman reboot.\" Expect intense action violence including killings, dead bodies, brutal beatings, shootings, explosions, and a severed thumb, plus sporadic strong language and disturbing dialogue about orphans and drug addiction (a fictional eye-drop drug with visibly addicted characters). Positive: Batman grapples with the costs of vengeance versus genuinely helping people, learns from his mistakes, and the film critiques how misinformation spreads online.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-batman","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":799,"t":"Thor: Love and Thunder","y":"2022","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Rescue adventure focuses on love; violence, language.\" Gorr the God Butcher kidnaps Asgard's children, and the opening shows a young girl die of starvation before Thor and Jane Foster (now Mighty Thor) mount a rescue; language includes several uses of \"s--t,\" and there are references to divine orgies and a played-for-laughs nude Thor. Positive: the story promotes teamwork, empathy, and choosing love over guarding your heart, with Valkyrie's bisexuality and Korg's two dads offering LGBTQ+ representation.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/thor-love-and-thunder","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Fantasy"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":800,"t":"Ant-Man and the Wasp: Quantumania","y":"2023","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"MCU threequel falls short on humor; violence, language.\" Scott and Hope, with Hank, Janet, and teenage Cassie, get stranded in the Quantum Realm battling Kang and his minions, with large-scale fights, background character deaths, and language like \"holy s--t\" and \"d--k\"; a tavern scene shows aliens drinking mind-altering beverages. Positive: the film promotes forgiveness, redemption, and \"looking out for the little guy,\" with the family working together and showing real courage.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ant-man-and-the-wasp-quantumania","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":801,"t":"Aquaman and the Lost Kingdom","y":"2023","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"\"Superhero sequel has comic-book violence, creepy monsters,\" says CSM. Concerns: largely bloodless but relentless action against skeletal zombies and clawed, tentacled creatures, plus a torture scene and a character who chooses suicide over capture; mild profanity including one use of \"goddamn\"; and Guinness beer shown in a positive light. Positive: Arthur shows courage and humility in asking a rival for help, with recurring themes of forgiveness, family, and working together against climate change.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/aquaman-and-the-lost-kingdom","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":802,"t":"Batman: The Doom That Came to Gotham","y":"2023","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Violence, drinking in animated Batman origin story,\" per Common Sense Media, this Lovecraftian tale has Bruce Wayne returning to Prohibition-era Gotham to stop a cult from unleashing an ancient evil. The review flags demonic and monster imagery throughout, a character sliced in half, a pickaxe attack, brief nonsexual nudity, hookah smoking, and a character known for binge-drinking wine, with mild profanity like \"son of a bitch.\" CSM found no clear positive messages or role models in this darker, horror-tinged origin story.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/batman-the-doom-that-came-to-gotham","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Horror"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":803,"t":"Blue Beetle","y":"2023","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Loving family, intense scenes in DC's Latino superhero tale.\" Concerns: intense mech-suit fighting and a disturbing possession-like sequence with screaming, red eyes, and something moving under a character's skin, plus a scene depicting an armed immigration raid; frequent mild-to-moderate language; and an extended kiss with a joke about male arousal. Positive: \"your family is your superpower\" -- the humble, family-devoted hero and his female relatives model intelligence and independence, in DC's first Latino-led superhero film.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/blue-beetle","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":804,"t":"Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3","y":"2023","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Trauma, teamwork at heart of darker MCU threequel.\" This installment is notably darker, with graphic violence (decapitations, blood, skeletal remains) and disturbing flashbacks of animal experimentation and torture on a young Rocket; language includes the MCU's first use of the F-word, and Peter gets severely drunk early on. Positive: the story is ultimately about found family, forgiveness, and moving forward from trauma, with the Guardians showing loyalty and self-sacrifice for one another.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/guardians-of-the-galaxy-vol-3","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":805,"t":"Legion of Super-Heroes","y":"2023","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Animated superhero origin tale has violence, some language,\" says Common Sense Media of this story, where Supergirl travels to the future to stop a terrorist group from stealing a powerful weapon. The review notes Batman choking a villain to death, another character ripped in half, Supergirl stabbed with a kryptonite knife, occasional profanity (\"s--t,\" \"son of a bitch\"), and ongoing sexual tension with passionate kissing between Supergirl and Brainiac 5. Positive: the film emphasizes teamwork as young heroes learn to value their own gifts.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/legion-of-super-heroes","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":806,"t":"Shazam! Fury of the Gods","y":"2023","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Funny superhero sequel has action violence, language.\" Concerns: collapsing buildings, monster attacks, impalings, zombie-like creatures, and a terrifying dragon; frequent profanity, including a child character's cut-off \"motherfu--\"; and a party scene with sparkling wine and an implied hangover. Positive: \"the most powerful thing about you is you\" -- themes of found family, teamwork, and courage, carried by a cast diverse in race, disability, body type, and background.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/shazam-fury-of-the-gods","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Comedy"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":807,"t":"The Flash","y":"2023","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Intense, long superhero adventure explores loss, teamwork,\" per CSM. Concerns: extensive comic book violence with explosions, military-grade weapons, and a fairly high body count, including the stabbing death of Barry's mother and a tense scene of babies falling from a crumbling hospital; frequent profanity including a prominent \"f--k\"; and a comedic scene of young Barry left nude from the waist down after super-speed strips his clothes. Positive: team-building and working together for the greater good, and learning to accept what can't be changed rather than tampering with the past.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-flash","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":808,"t":"The Marvels","y":"2023","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"\"Teamwork, action violence in fun, woman-centered MCU tale.\" Carol Danvers, Kamala Khan, and Monica Rambeau have their powers entangled and must team up against a Kree revolutionary, with laser-gun and spear battles and a few alarming scenes of planetary destruction; language is mild (\"s--t,\" \"damn,\" \"hell\"). Positive: the film celebrates the strength of female friendship and teamwork, with all three leads shown as brave, empathetic, and responsible, and a real emphasis on family acceptance.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-marvels","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":809,"t":"Justice League: Crisis on Infinite Earths Part One","y":"2024","la":"10+","ca":"10+","full":"This first chapter of a trilogy adapting the classic DC crossover mostly follows the Flash as the Justice League faces the android Amazo and a coming multiversal threat. Common Sense Media notes cartoon blood and violence with weapons, guns, explosions, and stabbing, along with language like \"s--t,\" \"crap,\" and \"goddamn,\" plus a married couple kissing. Positive: Barry Allen models teamwork and self-sacrifice, and the film's tagline -- \"the future isn't written until we write it\" -- underscores hope and perseverance.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/justice-league-crisis-on-infinite-earths-part-one","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":810,"t":"Justice League: Crisis on Infinite Earths Part Three","y":"2024","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Heroes unite for a universe-saving finale; intense action.\" This trilogy closer is heavier than typical Justice League fare: cartoon violence includes guns, explosions, and stabbings with visible blood, plus intense dinosaur-attack and bar-fight sequences, some profanity (\"s--t,\" \"hell\"), and characters drinking beer. Positive: Supergirl, Superman, and Wonder Woman risk everything for others, and the story leans hard on teamwork, sacrifice, and hope even as the universe collapses around them.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/justice-league-crisis-on-infinite-earths-part-three","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":811,"t":"Justice League: Crisis on Infinite Earths Part Two","y":"2024","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Superheroes fight evil in dark, violent sequel,\" says Common Sense Media of this middle chapter, as the Monitor rallies heroes while the multiverse unravels. The review cites cartoon blood and violence with guns, explosions, and stabbing, including a mind-controlled teen stabbing another with a pitchfork, plus language such as \"damn\" and \"bastard.\" Positive: characters like the Monitor and Wonder Woman model leadership and sacrifice, and the film keeps its throughline of hope and not giving up.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/justice-league-crisis-on-infinite-earths-part-two","genre":["Animation","Superhero","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Warner Bros. Animation","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":812,"t":"Madame Web","y":"2024","la":"12+","ca":"11+","full":"Common Sense Media's tagline is \"action violence in a female-centered Spidey superhero tale,\" recommending age 11+. Characters are shot, stabbed, strangled, and poisoned (with some blood but nothing graphic), and one scene has two characters waking up together in their underwear that turns threatening rather than romantic. Positive: paramedic Cassie's arc centers on protecting vulnerable teens, and CSM highlights the \"power of your mind has infinite potential\" message along with real teamwork among the female leads.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/madame-web","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Sony Pictures","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":813,"t":"Venom: The Last Dance","y":"2024","la":"14+","ca":"14+","full":"Common Sense Media's tagline calls it \"exposition, explosions in uninspired superhero threequel,\" with an age recommendation of 14+. Alien-monster attacks bring decapitations and blood, strong language includes \"motherf----r\" and repeated \"s--t,\" and Eddie gets visibly drunk in a bar scene. Positive: the film keeps circling back to Eddie and Venom's \"symbiotic\" partnership, framing their bond - however messy - as their real strength.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/venom-the-last-dance","genre":["Live-Action","Action","Superhero"],"studio":"Sony Pictures","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":814,"t":"Captain America: Brave New World","y":"2025","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"\"Action violence, swearing in solid, earnest MCU adventure.\" Sam Wilson steps fully into the Captain America role amid an international standoff and a rampaging Red Hulk, with hand-to-hand fights, assassination attempts, and explosions, plus regular profanity including \"s--t\" and \"son of a bitch.\" Positive: the film argues that courage and integrity, not superpowers, define a hero, and that people deserve second chances, with Sam modeling loyalty and doing what's right even under pressure.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/captain-america-brave-new-world","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":815,"t":"Superman","y":"2025","la":"12+","ca":"12+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Reboot mixes action violence, charming comic book energy.\" Concerns: large-scale destruction with implied civilian casualties and a shocking scene of a villain shooting an unarmed victim at close range in a game of Russian roulette; moderate profanity (\"s--t,\" \"bitch,\" \"goddamn\"); several passionate kisses between Clark and Lois, plus references to harems and implied sex workers; and Perry White frequently seen with a cigar. Positive: Superman models truth, justice, and using power responsibly, while Lois Lane brings intelligence, bravery, and loyalty to the story.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/superman","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":816,"t":"The Fantastic Four: First Steps","y":"2025","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"\"Marvel's First Family gets a retro, action-packed reboot.\" Set in a retro-futuristic alternate Earth, Reed, Sue, Johnny, and Ben face a planet-devouring cosmic villain, with super-powered battles, spacecraft crashes, one character's death (and a later revival), and mild language like \"bulls--t\" and \"hell.\" Positive: the film emphasizes teamwork, sacrifice, and using one's abilities to help others, with Sue Storm shown as intelligent, levelheaded, and fiercely protective as a new mother.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-fantastic-four-first-steps","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":817,"t":"Thunderbolts*","y":"2025","la":"13+","ca":"13+","full":"\"Trauma, violence in heartfelt MCU antihero adventure.\" A team of antiheroes and former villains, including Yelena Belova and the Winter Soldier, battle a shadowy government threat with intense combat (impalement, shooting), strong language, and frank references to depression, addiction, and past meth use. Positive: the film centers empathy and real connection as ways to cope with trauma and mental illness, with the team learning to support each other rather than fight alone.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/thunderbolts","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":818,"t":"Avengers: Doomsday","y":"2026","la":"12+","ca":"Not on CSM; substitute source used","full":"This crossover hasn't released yet (theaters December 2026), so no Common Sense Media review exists; per Wikipedia and early marketing, the Avengers, Wakandans, Fantastic Four, and X-Men band together against Doctor Doom (Robert Downey Jr.) as the multiverse collapses. Expect large-scale, PG-13-style superhero combat and peril consistent with prior Avengers films, with directors describing a more \"serious and somber\" tone than recent MCU entries. Positive: early descriptions point to themes of teamwork and unity across generations of heroes, though specific content details will need confirming once reviews are published.","srcUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avengers:_Doomsday","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"},{"num":819,"t":"Spider-Man: Brand New Day","y":"2026","la":"11+","ca":"11+","full":"\"Fourth MCU Spidey movie spins tale of action and emotion.\" Four years after the world forgot Peter Parker, he fights crime alone, facing frequent comic-book fighting and peril with bloody, oozing wounds, plus language like \"s--t\" and \"goddamn\" and a hot-tub scene with suggestive dialogue. Positive: the film's core message is that everyone deserves people to lean on, and Peter learns that isolating himself isn't healthy, with MJ's kindness and Ned's steady loyalty modeling real friendship.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/spider-man-brand-new-day","genre":["Live-Action","Superhero","Action"],"studio":"Marvel Studios","source":"MCUDC"}];
